# THE VERSE-DEV DRIVE: the charter staging runs.
#
# Two halves, six strata and twelve rules, in one file because they are one program: the REVIEW
# reads what has landed since it last looked, against the tree as it stands now, and remembers
# what it found; and the MERGE GATE turns what the review's fixes deliver into a proposal
# somebody decides. Install it whole —
#
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/comment-drift.tony
#
# — which installs the strata first whatever order they appear in (cmd/verse: "STRATA FIRST"),
# so the ordering is in force before a rule names it.
#
# The two halves keep their own numbering — the review's rules are 1..5, the gate's are 1..3
# with a 1b and 1c — because the prose in each refers to its own by number.
#
# comment-drift-states.md beside this reads the whole thing as state machines — which field each
# rule writes, and which arrows nothing draws — for a reader who wants the shape before the
# reasons.
#
# ── THE UNIT OF REVIEW IS A RANGE, and that is what changed ──────────────────────
#
# It reviewed main's TIP: one commit per push, whatever else the push carried. A push of three
# reviewed one of them, and a comment that started lying in the middle commit was never looked
# at. The obvious repair — review every commit — is worse, and the drive found out why before it
# was built: it reviews ancient commits whose tree no longer exists, files findings about symbols
# that have since moved, and proposes fixes against a checkout forty commits stale. The commit is
# the OCCASION to look; it is not the subject matter.
#
# So the unit is `last-reviewed..head`, reviewed against the tree at the far end. What that
# dissolves, all at once:
#
#   - no ancient commits: the range starts where the last review got to, and what it judges is
#     current code;
#   - no storm on first install: seed the cursor at the tip and the first range is empty;
#   - no linearity to reason about: a range is a TREE DIFF, so merges, fan-out at a fork and
#     non-fast-forward history are git's problem rather than the charter's;
#   - retry is free: a review that fails leaves the cursor where it was, so the next one covers
#     the same range plus whatever landed since. Delay makes the range bigger, not the backlog
#     longer.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated rather than discovered: per-commit attribution. A comment broken in one
# commit of a push and fixed in the next nets out and is never filed — which is correct, since
# the tree at the far end is what a comment has to be true about, but it is a behaviour change
# from reviewing each commit in turn.
#
# ── SEEDING, which is two writes and not one ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The cursor needs somewhere to stand, and where it stands is what the first range starts from:
#
#     verse data verse-dev.drift.at --put '{sha: <main's tip>, stage: idle}'
#
# Nothing fires until main moves, so a first install has no backlog whatever the source's window
# holds — which is the storm answered by construction rather than by a knob.
#
# ON A VERSE THAT RAN THE OLDER CHARTER there is one more step, and skipping it loses the ledger:
# findings written before this were addressed under (rule, subject, key) and the ones written
# after are addressed by symbol, so the old rows can never be closed by a verdict and would be
# gathered forever. Copy each open one to its symbol and remove the original:
#
#     verse data verse-dev.drift.finding.<symbol> --put '<its payload>'
#     verse data verse-dev.drift.finding.<old composed id> --rm

# ── THE REVIEW ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# THERE IS NO `reflected` STRATUM HERE ANY MORE, and its absence is the shape of the change: no
# rule in this half has a git entity as its SUBJECT. The tip is read — by a condition, which is
# what wakes these rules when it moves — and everything acted on belongs to this package. The
# gate half below still owns one, because a delivered branch is a git ref and that is its subject.
at: verse-dev.drift
reads: [ !verse-path [git] ]
---
# The cursor is one entity: where the review has got to. It BOUNCES — idle → claimed → idle —
# and a PATCH is what moves it, which is the one shape that can carry a moving value: a derive's
# address is a function of (rule, subject) and its content may reference identity and never
# state, precisely so that re-deriving writes the same bytes twice. A single slot everybody
# overwrites is what those two rules exist to forbid, so the cursor cannot be derived by anybody
# — it is patched, by rules whose subject it is (docs/examples/level-ping-pong.yaml: recursion
# inside one level is what the ordering permits).
- name: cursor
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, at]

# One entity per range, keyed by the commit it covers — its FAR end, and not the near one. The
# chain is serial, so `to` determines `from` and an id carrying both would be a separator
# convention to parse; and it is `to` that every reader downstream names, since `train-load` asks
# for the review of the tip main is standing on (`drift.range.<tip>.report`). `from` rides in the
# payload for whoever wants the span.
#
# It is also what gives each review its own TASK ID: a task's id is (rule, subject), so with the cursor as the subject every review would be
# `review-range@verse-dev.drift.at` — and a spawn on an id that already completed comes back
# instantly carrying the OLD answer, so the range is never looked at and the cursor advances past
# it (git issue 6j911yhzh12kswgjgsn0, found by watching exactly that happen).
- name: range
  level: 20
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, range]

- name: ledger
  level: 30
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]
---
# 1. THE CLAIM, and it is the whole race.
#
# The tip is read HERE, when the review is decided, and written down as `covering`. A commit
# landing while the review runs is then neither covered by it nor skipped: rule 5 advances to
# what was CLAIMED rather than to whatever the head says at completion, so the next range starts
# exactly where this one stopped.
#
# `sha: !not .[tip]` is condread.go's own example inverted — fire while the cursor is not
# standing on the tip — and the read joins this trigger's reads, so main moving is what wakes it.
# No `on:` clause: an `on` is a formula over the whole verse, and "a git head exists" rises once
# and then stands, which is a level rather than an edge and never fires again (trigger/edge.go).
- name: claim
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, at]
      value: !let
        let:
        - tip: !get-path
            at: [git, verse, head]
            path: sha
        in:
          stage: idle
          sha: !not .[tip]
  action:
    patch:
      stage: claimed
      covering: !get-path
        at: [git, verse, head]
        path: sha
---
# 2. …and the range it claimed becomes an entity, so the review has a subject of its own.
- name: open-range
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, at]
      value:
        stage: claimed
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, drift, range]
      id: $subject.value.covering
      value:
        from: $subject.value.sha
        to: $subject.value.covering
---
# 3. Before the review, the STANDING SET is read onto the range.
#
# A separate rule rather than a line in the next one, and the reason is ordering: a spawn's
# strings bind against the payload that FIRED the action, so an action that patched `outstanding`
# and then spawned would hand the task the payload from before its own write. Two firings, two
# commits, and the second sees the first. `stage` is the latch that sequences them.
#
# IT READS THE LEDGER, and that only works because rule 4 addresses a finding by its SYMBOL. A
# keyed fan-out lands under (rule, subject, key), and the subject is the review — so a later
# review's `resolved` used to land BESIDE the open row rather than on it, gathering `status:
# open` handed every stale claim back forever, and nothing a review said could ever close one.
# `id:` inside `!each` (c1231f7d) is what makes the address the place plus the symbol, so a
# verdict OVERWRITES the claim it is about.
#
# That is worth more than carrying the standing set from review to review, which was the other
# way to make closure work: carried forward, an answer REPLACES the set, so a reviewer going
# quiet about a finding deletes it. Here silence leaves it open — resolution stays a fact
# somebody wrote rather than an absence inferred from a model saying nothing.
#
# `status: open` is also the human override: set a finding's status to anything else and it stops
# being handed to the reviewer, with no charter change and nothing to argue with.
- name: gather
  stratum: range
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, range, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path stage
  action:
    patch:
      stage: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        at: [verse-dev, drift, finding, "*"]
        value:
          status: open
---
# 4. …and the range is reviewed, once, against what is already known.
#
# The latch is `stage: gathered`, which rule 3 writes and all three terminal hooks write past. A
# hook that did not would leave that ending unlatched, and the next thing to touch this entity
# would spawn again.
- name: review-range
  stratum: range
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, range, "*"]
      value:
        stage: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.coderunner
      # That environment pushes `refs/heads/verse/auto/$sha/$task`, so the deliverable is a diff
      # rather than prose about a diff. Delivery is the runner's act and the task only proposes
      # it, at the end; a run that changed nothing proposes nothing and leaves no branch.
      environment: verse-fix
      driver: llm
      grant: all
      vector: auto
      # THE ANSWER IS A CONTRACT rather than a request in the prose. "Your final message is JSON"
      # was obeyed on one run and ignored on the next, and an unparseable answer is not a bad
      # answer — it is no answer: `$data` resolves to nothing, the completion hook is refused,
      # and the review is lost with the record left in no state at all, silently (git issue
      # 91jktrr3h12ksa8fgsn0).
      answer:
        files: !irtype 0
        findings: !irtype []
      prompt: |
        The verse repository is checked out at ./verse with its full history, at $to.

        Review the RANGE $from..$to — everything that has landed since the last review — and
        answer with `at: $to`. The range is what you review; $to is the commit the tree stands
        at, and it is what your answer is a claim about. main can move while you work, so a
        reader must not have to assume which commit you meant.

        WHAT STILL STANDS. These are the findings the last review left open — claims that some
        Go symbol's comments disagree with its code:

        $outstanding

        A finding you do not mention stays open on its own and will be handed to you again, so
        leaving it out is correct and re-listing it unchanged costs tokens for no fact. What you
        answer with is what CHANGED: `status: resolved` for one that is right in the tree you are
        reading, and whatever this range broke.

        Check each carried finding against the TREE, not against your memory of the diff: a
        comment can be fixed by a commit that never touched the file it names, and the range is
        only the reason to look.

        WHAT CHANGED. The Go files the range touched:

            git -C verse diff --name-only $from..$to -- '*.go'

        For each of those files, read what changed in it:

            git -C verse diff $from..$to -- <path>

        and judge the comments on the declarations that changed, plus any comment the change
        made wrong elsewhere in the same file. A comment is STALE when it DISAGREES with the
        code it is attached to: a parameter that is gone, a rationale for a branch that was
        deleted, a "returns X" that returns Y, an algorithm described that was replaced under
        it. Terse, unfashionable and MISSING are not stale — staleness is a disagreement, not a
        gap.

        A range is not a commit, and that changes one thing: a comment broken by one commit and
        corrected by a later one in the SAME range is not a finding. What you are judging is the
        tree at $to, and the intermediate states are somebody else's history.

        FIX each stale one in place with write_file: rewrite the comment to say what the code now
        does, in the voice of the comments around it. Change nothing else — not the code, not the
        formatting, not a comment that is merely unlovely. Run no git command that writes: the
        working tree is the handoff, and delivery is the runner's.

        WHAT YOU DO NOT DO, because the working tree IS the deliverable and every file you leave
        behind is committed: do not write a test, do not run one, do not build, and do not add a
        file. You are judging whether a comment disagrees with the code beside it — never whether
        the code is right. A test you write to satisfy yourself lands in somebody's branch as
        though a reviewer had asked for it.

        A FIX YOU JUST WROTE RESOLVES NOTHING. Your edits are in a working tree that becomes a
        branch, and a branch is a PROPOSAL — somebody has to approve it, and it can be declined,
        or superseded, or retired unmerged while main moves on. So a symbol you fixed is still
        `status: open`, because it is still wrong in the tree you reviewed, and you say where you
        proposed the fix: `proposedAt: $to`. The review that comes after the fix LANDS reads
        main, finds the comment accurate, and resolves it. That is what makes the ledger a record
        of MAIN rather than of what agents believe they did.

        This is not hypothetical bookkeeping. A review fixed `task.Fatals`, marked it resolved,
        and its branch was retired before the merge; main kept the wrong comment while the ledger
        said somebody had dealt with it, and nothing re-examined it, because a resolved finding
        is not handed to anybody again.

        A finding you are handed that already carries `proposedAt` and is STILL WRONG means that
        fix did not land. Fix it again and say `proposedAt: $to` for this attempt: the work is
        cheap and idempotent, where a comment nobody corrects is permanent.

        READ EACH THING ONCE. Re-reading a file you have read does not make a verdict truer, and
        the second read costs a turn the first one already bought. When every changed declaration
        and every carried finding has a verdict, answer.

        Name each symbol the way Go does, repo-relative: `entity.nodeToPayload`,
        `source/github.Reflect`, `entity.(*LogdStore).Get`. That identity survives the comment
        being rewritten and the declaration moving between files, which a line number does not.
        `go doc ./<pkg> <Name>` says whether you have it right.

        YOUR FINAL MESSAGE IS THE ANSWER — a JSON document, nothing before it and nothing after
        it. Every key and every string is quoted, which is the whole of what has gone wrong
        before. Report an entry ONLY for a symbol that has something to say: a symbol you judged
        and found ACCURATE is not an entry, and an empty `findings` list is a real answer.

        EVERY FINDING CARRIES ALL FIVE KEYS — symbol, status, says, at, proposedAt — and
        `proposedAt` is `null` when you proposed no fix, which is every `resolved` one. Write the
        key with a null rather than leaving it out. An absent reference is an error where a null
        one is a value, so a finding missing the key fails the whole filing: not that finding —
        the WHOLE list, including the ones that were complete, and the review is lost with the
        range marked reviewed and the cursor advanced past it. That is not hypothetical; it is
        what the first run of this charter did (18:09Z 2026-09-10, `$item.proposedAt is not
        present in the payload that fired this action`).

            {
              "files": 2,
              "findings": [
                {"symbol": "entity.nodeToPayload",
                 "status": "open",
                 "says": "the comment says it returns nil for an empty map; it returns an empty payload",
                 "at": "$to",
                 "proposedAt": "$to"},
                {"symbol": "trigger.predicateNode",
                 "status": "resolved",
                 "says": "named a function deleted in this range; the comment is accurate now",
                 "at": "$to",
                 "proposedAt": null}
              ]
            }
    onDone:
      derive:
      - path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]
        value: !each
          over: $data.findings
          id: $item.symbol
          value:
            symbol: $item.symbol
            status: $item.status
            says: $item.says
            at: $item.at
            proposedAt: $item.proposedAt
      patch:
        stage: reviewed
        status: done
        report: $data
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        status: failed
        err: $err
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: !delete null
        err: !delete null
---
# 5. …and the cursor steps, but only onto the range it claimed and only once that range is done.
#
# `covering: !or .[done]` is the subject's own field tested against a bound set. The range's
# address cannot be read from a condition — a condition read may not name $subject, since the
# subject is what it selects — so this asks whether what we claimed is among the shas that have
# been reviewed. A failed review leaves `status: failed`, so it is not in that set: the chain
# stops where it broke, visibly, and `verse data verse-dev.drift.at --put '{stage: idle}'` is the
# retry, which by then covers everything since.
- name: advance
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, at]
      value: !let
        let:
        - done: !list-paths
            at: [verse-dev, drift, range, "*"]
            value:
              status: done
            path: to
        in:
          stage: claimed
          covering: !or .[done]
  action:
    patch:
      sha: $subject.value.covering
      covering: !delete null
      stage: idle
---

# ── THE MERGE GATE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# verse-dev drive: an agent's branch is a PROPOSAL, and somebody decides it.
#
# The coding runner delivers to `verse/auto/<sha>/<task>` (coderunner/workspace.go: that prefix
# is chosen so a developer who pulls and finds a commit nobody wrote can tell which system made
# it from the first two segments). This half is what consumes them: the branch becomes a fact,
# the fact carries a gate, and a yes merges it.
#
# WHAT DECIDES THE DESIGN is staleness. A `verse/auto/*` branch is cut from whatever main was
# when its task started, so by the time anybody answers, main may have moved. `base != tip` is
# the whole staleness predicate — no ancestry walk, no remote asked anything, no model — and a
# proposal that fails it is retired, cheaply, because the review that produced it is idempotent
# by construction.
#
# THE BATCH IS THE UNIT, not the branch. A proposal can only land while main is where it was cut
# from, so two outstanding proposals are two questions of which at most one can still be answered
# yes: approving either moots the other. `train` is one entity per tip; it collects what is
# deliverable and asks ONCE.

# ── AND THE GATE IS ITS OWN PACKAGE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It writes `merge:*` and reads two things: the source, and the review's ranges (rules 1c and 2b
# carry the review's report onto the proposal and the train). So the edge is merge → drift, and
# the graph is a chain rather than a loop — which is what verse checks, and what would refuse
# this file if the review ever came to read `merge:*` back.
at: verse-dev.merge
reads: [git, verse-dev.drift]
---
# The same reflected slice the review half no longer needs: here a git ref IS a subject.
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git

- name: merge
  level: 30
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, merge, proposal]

- name: train
  level: 40
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, merge, train]
---
# 1. A delivered branch becomes a proposal, and nothing else.
#
# TWO segments, not one: the push target is `verse/auto/<sha>/<task>` and `!glob` is
# filepath.Match, where `*` does NOT cross `/`. Written as `verse/auto/*` this matched nothing at
# all, and a selector that silently selects nothing is the failure this drive keeps finding.
#
# FACTS ONLY: a derive MERGES its literal fields every time it runs, so any field named here is
# restated on every re-derive. `state: open` dragged a merged proposal back to undecided and
# `base` tracked main's current tip, which is the left side of the staleness test comparing
# itself against the thing it is compared to. What is left is a function of the subject alone.
- name: proposal-appeared
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      # THIS repository's, because the git source mirrors more than one and each mirror's refs
      # stand under its own place: example/app's deliveries are named the same way and
      # belong to docs/working/signadot/pr.tony, which reaches them through an environment this
      # file's cannot.
      path: [git, verse, ref, "*"]
      value:
        name: !glob "verse/auto/*/*"
  action:
    derive:
    # THE BRANCH IS THE ID, said outright. A derive that does not name one is addressed by its
    # PROVENANCE — `<target>.<rule>.<subject…>`, so this one would land seven segments down at
    # `verse-dev.merge.proposal.proposal-appeared.git.verse.ref."verse/auto/…"` — and every rule below
    # that reads a proposal says `[verse-dev, merge, proposal, "*"]`, which is four. Those rules
    # would go on selecting, on the RULE level, whose payload is the whole subtree rather than
    # one proposal: `proposal-outrun` fired on it and asked the coderunner to retire a `$branch`
    # that node does not have.
    #
    # A branch name is unique per proposal and is what every reader keys on, so naming it here
    # says the identity the rest of the file already assumes. `train-cut` two rules down does
    # the same with a sha.
    - path: [verse-dev, merge, proposal]
      id: $subject.value.name
      value:
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        branch: $subject.value.name
        sha: $subject.value.sha
---
# 1b. …and the commit it was cut from is pinned, once. The latch applied to a field: it selects
# the proposals nobody has pinned, and writing one takes that proposal out of its own selection.
- name: proposal-based
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, merge, proposal, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path base
  action:
    patch:
      base: !get-path {at: [git, verse, head], path: sha}
---
# 1c. …and the proposal says what it is about, so the docket asks a real question rather than
# `whether open` about a ref name.
#
# `base` is main's tip when the branch appeared, which for a range review is that range's `to`
# unless main moved between the review answering and the push landing. The branch name carries
# the exact one — `verse/auto/<sha>/<task>` — but a charter has no string operations, so reading
# it back out is not sayable. The train below carries the exact one, and that is the copy a
# decider sees.
- name: proposal-says
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, merge, proposal, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path state
  action:
    patch:
      says: !get-path
        at: [verse-dev, drift, range, $subject.value.base]
        path: report
---
# 2. THE TRAIN: one entity per tip, so a new main is a new question and the old one stops asking.
- name: train-cut
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject: git.verse.head
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, merge, train]
      id: $subject.value.sha
      value:
        sha: $subject.value.sha
---
# 2b. …loaded with whatever is deliverable, and reloaded as that changes.
#
# THE READS ARE IN THE CONDITION BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT WAKES IT. A rule watches what its CONDITION
# reads; a read in the action is resolved when the action fires and opens no watch.
#
# `reviewed` is there for that reason and no other. The `says` read below is in the action, so a
# report landing never re-evaluated this rule: with the proposal derived before the review
# answered, the train sat loaded with nothing and nobody was asked — and when main next moved,
# the proposal was outrun and its branch deleted, so a real fix was discarded with nothing said.
# The old prose leant on "the re-fire a proposal brings", which holds only while a PERSON pushes
# the branches; the review pushes and then answers, so the report always lands last. Asking
# whether this tip is among the shas that have been REVIEWED puts the report in the read set,
# which is the wake-up. (The train's own sha cannot be an address in a condition: a condition
# read may not name $subject.)
#
# AND ONLY WHILE THE BATCH IS OPEN, and only for the tip that is actually there: a train whose
# gate has been answered is a batch somebody decided the contents of, and every superseded train
# would otherwise go on collecting and go on asking.
- name: train-load
  stratum: train
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, merge, train, "*"]
      value: !and
      - !not.has-path decided
      - !let
        let:
        - tip: !get-path {at: [git, verse, head], path: sha}
        - fresh: !list-paths
            at: [verse-dev, merge, proposal, "*"]
            value: !not.has-path state
            path: branch
        - reviewed: !list-paths
            at: [verse-dev, drift, range, "*"]
            value:
              status: done
            path: to
        in: !and
        - {sha: .[tip]}
        - {sha: !or .[reviewed]}
        - !not {branches: .[fresh]}
  action:
    patch:
      branches: !list-paths
        at: [verse-dev, merge, proposal, "*"]
        value: !not.has-path state
        path: branch
      # AND WHAT THE REVIEW SAID about the range this train's tip ends. The train knows the sha
      # outright, where a proposal knows the reviewed commit only as `base`.
      says: !get-path
        at: [verse-dev, drift, range, $subject.value.sha]
        path: report
---
# 2c. …and somebody decides the batch, once per tip.
#
# THE ADDRESS IS THE LATCH between tips and `decided` is the latch within one. The gate carries a
# deadline rather than waiting on a person indefinitely: `deadline: {within: "24h", default:
# deny}` settles on DENY when the clock runs out, which is the safe direction for the one action
# here with a consequence nobody can quietly undo.
#
# AN EMPTY TRAIN ASKS NOTHING. `onDenied` writes `decided` too, because a deny IS a decision
# about this main: without it the refused firing writes nothing and the next commit asks the same
# person the same question about the same batch.
- name: train-run
  stratum: train
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, merge, train, "*"]
      value: !and
      - !has-path branches
      - !not {branches: []}
      - !not.has-path decided
      - !let
        let: [{tip: !get-path {at: [git, verse, head], path: sha}}]
        in: {sha: .[tip]}
  gates:
  - name: merge-to-main
    mode: ask
    deadline: {within: "24h", default: deny}
    onDenied:
      patch:
        decided: declined
  action:
    patch:
      decided: landing
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.coderunner
      environment: verse-merge
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      # A STATIC LIST OVER A DYNAMIC SET: the branches are not interpolated into the command,
      # they are FOUND in the clone. The train's `branches` field is for the person reading the
      # docket; the task reads the refs it has.
      #
      # EACH CAR IS REBASED ONTO THE TASK BRANCH BEFORE IT IS FAST-FORWARDED IN, and without that
      # the batch is nominal: the first `--ff-only` moves the branch and every sibling after it
      # is then neither ancestor nor descendant and is refused. A train of N landed exactly ONE
      # (issue ezh7m9akh12ksde5g9n0). A rebase that cannot apply is aborted and that car is
      # skipped — safe here because a comment fix is a textual edit localized to the symbol in
      # the finding, so there is no judgment for a conflict to need.
      calls:
      - tool: run_bash
        input:
          command: "tb=$(git -C verse rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD); for b in $(git -C verse for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/remotes/mirror/verse/auto/*/*'); do git -C verse checkout -B _car \"$b\" || continue; if git -C verse -c user.name='verse coderunner' -c user.email=coderunner@invalid rebase \"$tb\"; then git -C verse checkout \"$tb\" && git -C verse merge --ff-only _car; else git -C verse rebase --abort || true; git -C verse checkout \"$tb\"; fi; done"
      - tool: push
        input: {destination: main}
    onDone:
      patch:
        decided: landed
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        decided: failed
        err: $err
    # A cancel decided NOTHING, so `decided` is unwritten and the gate is asked again — where a
    # FAILED run should not re-ask anybody anything, which is why onFailed leaves it written.
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        decided: !delete null
        err: !delete null
---
# 3. Main moved, so the proposals cut from where it used to be can no longer land — and each
# one's branch is retired.
#
# What made it sayable is a condition that can read another entity: `!get-path` reads main's tip
# from the same snapshot this condition is evaluated against, `!let` binds it, and `!not` compares
# the subject's own field against it. The read joins what this trigger READS, so main moving
# re-evaluates the rule.
#
# Ungated, deliberately, and it is the one call here worth arguing with. Deleting a
# `verse/auto/*` branch destroys nothing that cannot be remade. Against that, a gate on every
# stale branch fills the docket with questions whose only honest answer is yes, and a docket
# nobody can keep up with is one nobody reads.
- name: proposal-outrun
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, merge, proposal, "*"]
      value: !and
      - !not.has-path state
      - !let
        let: [{tip: !get-path {at: [git, verse, head], path: sha}}]
        in: {base: !not .[tip]}
  action:
    patch:
      state: stale
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.coderunner
      environment: verse-merge
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      calls:
      - tool: push
        input: {destination: retire, ref: $branch}
      # No `answer:`, and that is the driver rather than an omission: a call list has no model
      # to repair a contract with, so a spawn carrying one is refused. What used to be read out
      # of the answer — which branches went — is the LIST ITSELF, and the ref is in the record
      # before the task starts.
    #
    # The patch above already wrote `stale`, so a failed retirement lands on `stale` with an
    # `err` beside it: the proposal IS stale whether or not its branch could be removed, and the
    # branch outliving it is a ref nobody reads rather than a decision anybody is waiting on.
    onFailed:
      patch:
        err: $err
